llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): reserve a
multimodal projector's memory on the device it runs on when fitting the model.

WHY
---
When llama-server fits a model to free VRAM (the default), it adds the
projector's worst-case memory to the free-memory margin it keeps per device.
It looks the projector's device up in the ggml backend registry and uses that
registry index as the index into fit_params_target. That vector is indexed by
the model's device list, which is a different list: the registry also holds
the CPU, and Ollama loads its backends dynamically with the CPU first. On a
one-GPU machine the result is inverted:

- projector on the GPU: its memory goes to index 1, which is no device, so
  nothing is reserved for it and the fit fills the GPU as if the projector
  were not there. Encoding an image then needs memory that is not free.
- projector on the CPU (--no-mmproj-offload): its host memory goes to index
  0, the GPU, so about 1.1 GB of VRAM is held back for nothing.

Seen with Qwen3.5 9B and its 0.9 GB vision projector on an 8 GB RX 590: with
the projector on the GPU the fit put all layers on the GPU, and a 1080p image
then took the card to 8074 of 8192 MiB. On a card that also drives the
desktop, the compositor failed to pin its framebuffers and applications lost
their GPU contexts. Nothing here is gfx803-specific.

WHAT
----
The projector's per-device memory is matched against the model's device list:
--device when given, otherwise the order the model uses (RPC servers, then
discrete GPUs, then integrated GPUs only when there is no discrete one).
Memory on a device outside that list, such as the CPU, has no fit target and
is not added. Same model and 8k context on the RX 590: projector on the GPU,
the margin becomes 1024 + 1149 MiB and 30 of 33 layers are offloaded;
projector on the CPU, the margin stays 1024 MiB and all 33 are offloaded.

